Usage

Supported architectures: amd64, arm64, armv7, hppa, i386, macppc, octeon,
powerpc64, riscv64, and sparc64.

Run a VM:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-d directory] [-f image] [-H hostname] [-p port] \
        <arch> [qemu args ...]

Boot installation media with the target VM disk attached:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] [-H hostname] \
        -m (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Autoinstall or autoupgrade over TFTP:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] [-H hostname] \
        -i (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]
    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-k] [-d directory] [-f image] [-H hostname] \
        -u (-s|-R version) <arch> [installurl] [qemu args ...]

Use -m for hppa, macppc, powerpc64, and sparc64; -i and -u are available
for the remaining architectures.

Use -s for a snapshot or -R version for a release. The optional
installurl selects a distribution mirror or local directory.

Use -n to print commands instead of running them.

Use -k to bypass signature verification on the host.

Use -H hostname[.domain] to override obsd-<arch>.<host hostname>.
A short name uses the host's hostname as its domain.

Use -p port in normal run mode to forward a host port to the guest's SSH
port; no host SSH port forwarding is bound by default.

Use -f image before <arch> to select the qcow2 disk in any mode;
the default is <arch>.qcow2 in working directory.

Use -d directory to change the working directory; relative paths,
including -f and a local installurl, are resolved there.

DISK_SIZE env sets the size of new VM disks; the default is 128G.


Installation configuration

Installation inputs are install.conf, disklabel, site/, site.perms,
and the Octeon installation pflash. If any exist in the working
directory, use it for the whole set; otherwise use
/usr/local/share/examples/obsd.sh. Files are never mixed between them.

-i requires install.conf; -u requires it and an existing disk.
-m requires install.conf or an existing disk. -i and -m create
missing disks; run and -z require an existing disk.

upgrade.conf uses install.conf. Provide disklabel when the answers
reference it.

Optional site/ and site.perms must be provided together. bsdtar builds
site.tgz from the mtree manifest, with contents= paths relative to
site/. For example:

    #mtree
    /set type=file uid=0 gid=0 mode=0644
    ./etc type=dir mode=0755
    ./etc/example.conf mode=0600 contents=etc/example.conf
    ./install.site mode=0755 contents=install.site

The HTTP helper and OpenBIOS files come from beside obsd.sh, otherwise
/usr/local/share/obsd.sh. Temporary installation files are removed
when QEMU exits.


Running as a service

Install obsd.rc as /etc/rc.d/obsd and obsd.login as
/etc/login.conf.d/obsd. The service defaults to user _vmd and directory
/var/vm; that directory must exist and be writable by _vmd.

The example login class uses datasize=20G: 16G for the default guest
RAM, 1G for the shared TCG translation cache, and 3G for other QEMU
allocations. The 3G allowance is an estimate and may need adjustment
for the workload.

Create an Octeon service instance:

    doas ln -s /etc/rc.d/obsd /etc/rc.d/obsd_octeon
    doas cp /etc/login.conf.d/obsd /etc/login.conf.d/obsd_octeon
    doas sed -i 's/^obsd:/obsd_octeon:/' /etc/login.conf.d/obsd_octeon
    doas rcctl enable obsd_octeon
    doas rcctl set obsd_octeon flags "-p 22222 octeon -m 12G"

The login class entry must match the service name. These flags forward
host TCP port 22222 to the guest's SSH port and give the guest 12G of
RAM. The tmux socket uses the full service name, so this instance uses
/var/vm/obsd_octeon.sock.

Install a snapshot VM using the service's user and login class:

    doas su -fl -c obsd_octeon -s /bin/sh _vmd -c \
        '/usr/local/bin/obsd.sh -d /var/vm -s -i octeon -m 12G'

After installation, start the service:

    doas rcctl start obsd_octeon

Connect to its console:

    doas -u _vmd tmux -N -S /var/vm/obsd_octeon.sock attach-session

Detach with Ctrl-b d to leave the VM running.

To investigate a failed start, use rcctl -d. The debug start retains
the tmux pane if the VM command exits with an error. Attach to the
console as above, or print its output and exit status:

    doas rcctl -d start obsd_octeon
    doas -u _vmd tmux -N -S /var/vm/obsd_octeon.sock capture-pane -p -S -

A retained pane still counts as a running session. After inspection,
stop the service before retrying:

    doas rcctl stop obsd_octeon


Architecture limits

On arm64, armv7, and riscv64, -i and -u may boot an existing system;
select network boot manually in U-Boot.

On powerpc64, -m may require selecting "OpenBSD install" in Petitboot.
Swapping SATA ports does not reliably select the installer.

On armv7, the MSDOS boot partition appears as i: in the disklabel;
autopartitioning has room for at most 14 OpenBSD partitions.

On macppc, -m boots cd*.iso; use one CPU and at most 2G of RAM.

On octeon, normal run uses u-boot-octeon3-obsd-boot.pflash from the
working directory, then examples. Installation uses
u-boot-octeon3-obsd-install.pflash from the selected project directory.
Example pflash is read only; local pflash is writable.

Both environments use four CPUs. To change this, copy the relevant
pflash locally, edit and save bootcmd's numcores arguments in U-Boot,
and pass matching -smp arguments.


Image maintenance

Shrink a qcow2 image:

    ./obsd.sh [-n] [-d directory] [-f image] -z <arch> \
        [qemu-img convert args ...]

Add -c after <arch> to create a compressed qcow2 image. To reclaim
unused space, first boot the guest with /bsd -s and zero free space:

    fsck -p && mount -uw / && mount -a -t ffs &&
        mount -t ffs | while read dev on mp rest; do
            dd if=/dev/zero of=$mp/.zero bs=1m
            rm -f $mp/.zero
        done
    sync
    halt
